# Initialization

Initialize your device, connect it to Pontia Cloud, and open the Remote Dashboard to start working with your agents. Choose the interactive wizard for guided setup, or use separate commands when an agent or script needs to perform each step explicitly.

Check the [installation requirements](/docs/getting-started) and install the Agent Clients you want to use. Run setup as the operating-system user who will run those clients. Local service management requires Linux with a running systemd user service manager, or macOS 13.5 or later.

## Interactive initialization

With Node.js and npm installed, run:

```sh
npx @pontia/cli init
```

This installs Pontia if needed and starts the wizard. If Pontia is already on your `PATH`, you can also run `pontia init` directly.

The wizard lets you:

1. Select the Client integrations to install: Pi, Codex, Claude Code, or any combination. Codex includes its daemon startup and [launcher](/docs/agent-clients/codex#codex-launcher).
2. Review and confirm the setup.
3. Create or retain the local API token, start the Pontia service, and enable the selected Clients.
4. Sign in to Pontia Cloud and register the device for remote access.

When prompted to sign in and enable remote access, accept and complete the browser authorization. Select an available edge and confirm the device name. Pontia opens the Remote Dashboard when setup succeeds. Follow the [Dashboard guide](/docs/dashboard) to start your first Session.

Existing configuration and Workspace Browser roots are retained. Without configured roots, the Workspace Browser defaults to your home directory.

Run `pontia init` again to check and repair your installation. The first run selects recommended Clients; later runs default to your enabled Clients. Newly selected Clients are configured and enabled. Unchecking a Client skips it for this run and keeps its settings and installation. Healthy integrations are reused. On an existing installation, Pontia checks and starts its service before the Client selection; cancelling Client setup leaves that service available.

## Configure or repair a Client later

Run `pontia setup` to select Clients, or specify one directly:

```sh
pontia setup pi
pontia setup codex
pontia setup claude
```

Each command applies the recommended local configuration and checks the installation. Existing valid configuration is reused and missing integration files are restored. After the Pontia service starts, the selected Clients are enabled.

Selected Client runtime paths are saved for the Pontia service. Explicit environment paths take precedence over saved paths. The service restarts when its configuration changes. For Codex, open a new terminal or run the printed shell activation command afterward.

<h2 id="non-interactive-initialization">Non-interactive initialization</h2>

Follow these steps in order, including the linked instructions for your platform. Each step includes commands and the results to check before continuing.

Client authentication and Cloud login may require user authorization. Complete the authorization when prompted, then continue setup.

### 1. Prepare the installation

Choose the Clients to enable (Pi, Codex, Claude Code, or a combination), the device display name, and the project directories to browse. Run every step as the same operating-system user. Install and enable Grok Build as described in its [Agent Client guide](/docs/agent-clients/grok).

1. Install `pontia` and `pontiad` with `npx @pontia/cli install` and add `$HOME/.local/bin` to your `PATH`, or use the [shell installer for Linux](/docs/platforms/linux#install-with-the-shell-script) or [macOS](/docs/platforms/macos#install-with-the-shell-script) without running the interactive initializer. For missing Client tools, follow the official [Pi quickstart](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/quickstart), [tmux installation guide](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Installing), or [Codex CLI installation](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/codex/cli). Install only the selected Clients and their prerequisites; reuse working installations.
2. Select one absolute `PONTIA_HOME` for all commands. Preserve an existing installation's configuration and credentials. The default is `~/.pontia`.
3. For Codex, select its existing authenticated home: the configured `CODEX_HOME`, or `~/.codex` by default. Use this same directory for Codex authentication, its daemon, and Pontia.

For the default paths, or to retain paths already set in the shell:

```sh
export PONTIA_HOME="${PONTIA_HOME:-$HOME/.pontia}"
pontia --version
```

If using Codex, also set:

```sh
export CODEX_HOME="${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}"
```

Use these environment values in every shell used for setup. Substitute your actual paths and IDs in the examples below.

### 2. Install and verify the Client integrations

Install and enable the integrations for your selected Clients here.

Run only the commands for your selected Clients:

```sh
pontia setup pi
pontia setup codex
pontia setup claude
```

Pi requires an installed `pi`; its setup installs `@pontia/pi-client-plugin`. Codex requires an installed CLI with daemon support; its setup configures autostart, verifies the connection, and installs the launcher and shell PATH. Claude Code requires Node.js LTS. Setup installs the default host at the same version as Pontia, or checks the host you explicitly selected.

These commands save runtime paths, configure the Pontia service, and enable the selected Clients. For Codex, open a new terminal or run the printed activation command. Complete authentication using each Client's own login flow; setup does not sign in or change model credentials, sandbox settings, or approval policy.

<h3 id="start-the-local-service">3. Configure and start the local service</h3>

Merge your listen address, Workspace Browser roots, and Client executable settings into `$PONTIA_HOME/config.toml`, retaining existing settings you want to keep. See [Configuration](/docs/configuration) for the available options.

Use the same absolute `PONTIA_HOME` for every command. Store persistent service settings in `config.toml`.

```sh
pontia up
pontia status
```

`pontia up` creates or retains the API token in `config.toml`, installs or refreshes the per-user service, waits for it to become healthy, and prints the Local Dashboard URL. It restarts the service if it is already running. The printed URL contains the local API token; keep it private.

### 4. Align Client settings

Now list the available Client entries:

```sh
pontia client list
```

Confirm that each selected entry shows `enabled`. If the Pontia service cannot find `pi`, see [Override the Pi command](/docs/agent-clients/pi#override-the-pi-command). Step 7 verifies these Clients by starting Sessions from the Remote Dashboard.

### 5. Sign in to Pontia Cloud

Sign in to authorize device registration with Pontia Cloud:

```sh
pontia login
```

Open the printed verification URL, sign in, and approve the one-time code. The command completes when authorization succeeds. When an agent runs setup, it can share the URL and code with you while waiting for approval.

You can reuse an existing valid Cloud login; `pontia remote edges` in the next step confirms access. Keep the saved credential in `auth.json` private.

### 6. Register the device without prompts

List the edges available to the logged-in user:

```sh
pontia remote edges
```

The command outputs a JSON array of objects with `id` and `name` fields. Choose an available edge and pass its ID together with your device name:

```sh
pontia remote enable --edge <EDGE_ID> --name "Development workstation"
pontia doctor
```

Replace `<EDGE_ID>` with an ID from the list and supply both `--edge` and `--name` for non-interactive registration.

Registration creates the device identity and updates the running local service. An already registered device keeps its existing name and edge. To rename it, use `pontia remote edit --name "New name"`.

### 7. Verify the Cloud-connected device

Run:

```sh
pontia status
pontia client list
pontia doctor
```

Confirm the following results:

- The local service is running and HTTP health is healthy.
- Every selected Client entry is enabled.
- The diagnostic report confirms a registered device and a matching local encryption identity.

Open the Remote Dashboard URL from `pontia doctor`. Follow the [Dashboard guide](/docs/dashboard) to activate a Workspace and start a Session with each selected Client. A successful Session confirms that the device is reachable and the Client is ready to use.

For agent-assisted setup, finish with a summary of the configured Clients, service health, device name, Remote Dashboard URL, and verification results. If browser verification remains, include the URL and the steps above so you can complete it. See [Remote access](/docs/remote-access) and [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) for help.
